+\chapter{PHM Case Study: Target Construction and Condition Prediction} \label{ch:phm} Across twenty gear runs in three held-out experiments, combining three groups of sensor features gave a mean absolute error of 0.8238 percentage points against a photographic condition proxy. A predictor that returned the training mean gave an error of 0.8042. Both errors measure agreement with an image-processing rule applied to post-run tooth photographs. The records contain no independent measurement of the damaged area against which to validate that rule.
+In retrospect, PHM serves as a methodological negative control for the wider fusion comparison. Even a reduction in prediction error would need independent validation before it could be interpreted as better damage measurement. The study therefore distinguishes changes in the target from changes in the predictor. Image-only and supplementary experiments had different targets or assessment populations. Under the official challenge protocol, periodic photographs were available during training, whereas deployment relied on sensor measurements \citep{PHM2026Challenge}.
 \begin{figure}[htbp] \caption{PHM Scalar Regression From Post-Run Photographs}\label{fig:phm-route} \centering\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/phm_executed_route.png} \displaynote{The extraction code adds latency to the 768 image features, producing a 769-dimensional input to regression.} \end{figure} \section{Study Objective and Relationship to the Research Questions} For SQ1, the image and sensor experiments assessed whether frozen visual features and temporal sensor representations could reproduce a specified target. For SQ2, the principal fusion comparison combined context, root-mean-square (RMS) vibration and condition indicators, all available at inference. It did not test joint image--sensor inference. For SQ3, a predictor that already returns nearly constant values can appear insensitive to corruption, so its clean-input error matters. For SQ4, the training-mean comparison asks whether measured sensor inputs improved prediction over a rule that ignores them at inference. It cannot establish whether the photographic target measures damage. That question requires a physical reference independent of the rule.\ev{PHM target and deployment inputs}{\EvidenceRef{E021}{}}
 \section{Dataset Overview and Acquisition Structure} The retained population contains EXP-A runs 1--5, EXP-B runs 1--7 and EXP-F runs 1--8: twenty nominal run units across three experiments. A run can contain multiple tooth photographs, camera-sequence views, high-frequency vibration members, low-frequency context measurements and condition-indicator records. An image, a minute-level feature row and a run are consequently different observational units. Repeated views and long sensor sequences increase the number of stored observations without increasing the number of independently acquired experiments to the same extent.
 Experiment and run identifiers are retained through derived manifests. Tooth identity and image role define target generation, whereas archive/member identity and measurement provenance define sensor features. Their correspondence is principally at run level. This supports joining measurements to a post-run proxy, rather than asserting hardware-synchronized image/sensor events or prospective image availability. Evidence covers these manifests and inspected implementation, with incomplete independent reinspection of raw archive contents.\ev{PHM experiments and acquisition sources}{\EvidenceRef{E020}{}}
